Multi-stage Docker Build

Scene Start:
Interviewer ne bola:
 “Have you worked with multi-stage builds in Docker?”
Tumhare mann mein pehla sawaal:
 “Ye multi-stage actual me hota kya hai?”


Let’s create a real-world relatable scene:
Scene: You're cooking Maggi
Imagine you’re cooking in your kitchen — You boil water, add masala, cook noodles.

Now picture this:
You invite friends to eat.
But instead of just serving the Maggi,
You hand them gas cylinder, stove, kadhai, masale, sab kuch.

They’ll be like:
“Bhai, mujhe bas Maggi chahiye thi — ye poora kitchen kyu de diya?” 

That’s what happens when you don’t use multi-stage builds in Docker.


You give your app plus all the build tools, caches, source code — everything!


Docker Build without Multi-stage:
dockerfile
 FROM node:18
 WORKDIR /app
 COPY . .
 RUN npm install
 RUN npm run build
 CMD ["npm", "start"]

Ye kya karega?
Sab kuch ek hi image me hai
Final image 900 MB+
Production me dev tools bhi ja rahe hain 




Now let’s fix it — Multi-stage style:
dockerfile

# Stage 1: Build
 FROM node:18 AS builder
 WORKDIR /app
 COPY . .
 RUN npm install
 RUN npm run build

# Stage 2: Serve
 FROM nginx:alpine
 COPY --from=builder /app/build /usr/share/nginx/html

Ab kya ho raha?
Stage 1: Tum kitchen me Maggi paka rahe ho
Stage 2: Sirf plate me Maggi serve ho rahi hai
Final image size? Just 20-30 MB
No extra tools. Secure, clean, fast!


Why it matters?
Smaller image = Faster deploy
No extra baggage = Secure prod
Better CI/CD = Happy DevOps team 


In Interview, say this:
We use multi-stage builds to keep our production Docker images lightweight. We separate the build and run steps — only shipping what's needed in prod. It improves security and speeds up deployments.



"Don’t send the whole kitchen. Just serve the dish!"
 That's multi-stage Docker build.
